# At the CONTEXT root, which is the only place Docker looks.
#
# This file started life at apps/api/.dockerignore, next to the Dockerfile,
# which felt tidy and does nothing: Docker reads .dockerignore from the root of
# the build context, and the context here is the repository. Everything below
# was therefore being sent to the daemon and copied into the image.
#
# Two consequences, one slow and one serious. The host's node_modules — built
# for whatever machine ran the build — landed on top of the ones installed
# inside the image. And `.env` went into a layer, where a later `rm` does not
# remove it.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
.turbo
coverage
*.log
.git
.gitignore
.DS_Store
backups
/tmp
